; compare_old.writ — a tiny recoverable machine. `mode` walks working -> broken
; -> working (via `restore`) -> safe (via `finish`). Because `restore` lets a
; broken machine climb back to working and then finish, EVERY reachable
; situation can still reach `safe`, so the `live` property `still-safe`
; (compare_old.claims) HOLDS.
;
; compare_new.writ is this model MINUS `restore`: broken then becomes a trap that
; can never reach `safe`, so `still-safe` FAILS there. `writ compare` reports the
; guarantee LOST and exits 1. (Mirrors the any_model/captured_trap trap at small
; scale: delete the recovery move, lose the liveness guarantee.)
;
; `shadow` is a second, never-written arrow so the schema carries a real
; `mode-tracks-shadow` equation; identical in old and new, it reports preserved.

(load "stdlib.writ")   ; `=` is a stdlib form (§8.6); there is no implicit prelude

(schema sys
  (type mode-t (working broken safe))
  (type machine
    (arrow mode   (to mode-t))
    (arrow shadow (to mode-t)))
  (equation mode-tracks-shadow (= machine.mode machine.shadow)))

(instance day-one sys
  (machine m (mode working)
             (shadow working)))

(use sys)
(initial day-one)

(transition break   (when (is m.mode working)) (do (set m.mode broken)))
(transition restore (when (is m.mode broken))  (do (set m.mode working)))
(transition finish  (when (is m.mode working)) (do (set m.mode safe)))
